itrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_026\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Aminoprop-2-enoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Dehydroalanine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_027\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"Dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_028\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-Aminopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"Alanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO2/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h2H,4H2,1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct glycolaldehyde Strecker formation of serine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed ambient operating window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, concentrated aqueous carbonyl/ammonia/cyanide mixture; approximately pH 9.2; limit aldehyde residence before cyanide capture.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to glycolaldehyde and forms an imine or iminium species; cyanide addition establishes the serine carbon skeleton. Water is the condensation coproduct.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the best-resolved carbon-skeleton construction for serine. Miller (1957) established the general aldehyde/HCN Strecker mechanism, and Ritson et al. (2018) demonstrated rapid serine-aminonitrile formation in the protected glycolaldehyde system. The reported approximately two-hour conversion applies specifically to that protected experiment, not necessarily to every unprotected mixture. Glycolaldehyde degradation competes, and no mineral catalyst is required.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydration of serine aminonitrile to serinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved proton activity provides general acid catalysis; no mineral is assigned.\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-363 K; proposed natural-hydrolysis window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"General acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Transfer to acidic water, provisionally pH 1-3; prolonged residence; reduced UV exposure through depth or shading. This is not a demonstrated rate prescription.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acid-assisted water addition to the nitrile gives an imidic intermediate that tautomerizes to the primary amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"An amide stage is chemically expected in nitrile hydrolysis. Miller (1957) supports aminonitrile hydrolysis in discharge-product chemistry, while Xu et al. (2018) and Ritson and Sutherland (2023) establish serine aminonitrile rather than a continuous mild route to free serine. The explicit serinamide node and proposed natural conditions are mechanistic extrapolations, not a reported isolated intermediate and measured environmental rate. This is a principal network bottleneck.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Serinamide hydrolysis to free serine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved proton activity provides general acid catalysis.\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-363 K; proposed natural-hydrolysis window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"General acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic aqueous residence followed by cooling; ammonia product is predominantly ammonium at the proposed pH. Residence time and serine survival require experimental determination.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water attacks the protonated amide carbonyl; collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate releases ammonia/ammonium and forms the carboxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This completes conventional aminonitrile-to-amino-acid hydrolysis, consistent with Miller (1957). Its integration with modern serine-nitrile photochemistry is not established by the supplied experiments, so the specified natural completion remains an extrapolation. Analytical strong-acid hydrolysis must not be equated with this environmental scenario. Cooling limits the destruction documented by Andersson and Holm (2000).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Reversible bisulfite storage of glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_016\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aqueous bisulfite is a stoichiometric reversible trapping reagent, represented by mol_010, not a catalyst.\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350 K; surface concentration and storage scenario\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 6.5; aqueous capture with optional evaporation and rewetting. Formulas use neutral-parent representations of the actual ionic adducts.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Bisulfite adds nucleophilically to the aldehyde carbonyl to form a hydroxysulfonate reservoir.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson et al. (2018) recovered more than 90% of glycolaldehyde as its adduct or hydrate after their protected dry-down/reconstitution procedure, unlike the unprotected control. This branch has a chemical storage function rather than merely adding connectivity. Compatibility with residual upstream photoredox reagents must still be tested.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonia incorporation into the protected glycolaldehyde reservoir\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed ambient window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Raise the protected solution to approximately pH 9.2 with available ammonia/ammonium buffering; maintain anoxic storage.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible carbonyl-adduct exchange, imine formation and sulfite capture establish the amino-hydroxysulfonate. The equation is a net transformation, not a claim of concerted substitution.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson et al. (2018) identified a stable 1-amino-2-hydroxyethylsulfonate reservoir in glycolaldehyde/ammonia/bisulfite chemistry, persisting during a seven-day storage test. This separates preservation from subsequent cyanide addition without assigning an unsupported mineral catalyst.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide conversion of the protected amino adduct to serine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_017\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; proposed ambient window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 9.2 after cyanide addition; the cited experiment reported smooth conversion within about two hours.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reversible sulfite loss exposes an imine/iminium equivalent that is trapped by cyanide, regenerating the dissolved S(IV) reservoir.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is directly supported by Ritson et al. (2018). Bisulfite acts as a reversible protecting reagent and is released during product formation; it is not a heterogeneous catalyst. Efficient formation of serine aminonitrile does not establish subsequent free-serine accumulation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"N-Formylation of serine aminonitrile in formamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_018\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Formamide is the concentrated medium and formyl donor, represented by mol_018, not a catalyst.\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370 K; candidate surface window, with kinetics requiring verification\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Formamide-rich, low-water-activity phase after concentration; dissolved salts and residual water may remain. The supplied summary does not specify the study's exact temperature.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net transfer of a formyl group from formamide to the aminonitrile amino group releases ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Green and Russell (2023) demonstrated N-formylated serine-nitrile chemistry in formamide. The derivative is therefore a supported molecular branch, but concentration of formamide at a natural site and operation throughout the proposed surface window remain uncertain. N-Formylation is not evidence that the derivative subsequently yields free serine efficiently.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate hydrolysis of N-formylserine aminonitrile to N-formylserine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved acid provides hydrolysis catalysis.\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-363 K; speculative environmental operating window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"General acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Rehydration into acidic water, provisionally pH 1-3; prolonged residence. The ordering and selectivity of nitrile hydrolysis versus deformylation are unknown.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net nitrile-to-carboxylic-acid hydrolysis through an amide stage, with temporary retention of the N-formyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative completion of the precursor chemistry demonstrated by Green and Russell (2023). Ordinary nitrile hydrolysis makes this chemically possible, but the supplied research does not establish selective accumulation of N-formylserine from this substrate under natural conditions. Deformylation, dehydration and other losses may outrun this branch; it is included as a testable route, not a demonstrated serine yield.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Candidate deformylation of N-formylserine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_021\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved acid catalyzes cleavage of the formamide linkage.\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-363 K; speculative environmental operating window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"General acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic rehydration followed by cooling; no claim of measured rate or selective serine recovery.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of the N-formyl amide releases serine and formic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Chemically conventional deprotection, but speculative as the environmental endpoint of the Green and Russell (2023) route. The supplied work supports the N-formyl aminonitrile precursor, not this complete mild sequence to free serine. This step explicitly prevents a protected derivative from being mislabeled as the target.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic aqueous formaldehyde/cyanide inventory; mildly acidic to mildly alkaline conditions with sufficient cyanide nucleophile; concentration and HCN volatilization are important.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide adds to formaldehyde, followed by proton transfer, producing the C2 cyanohydrin glycolonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This established entry reaction connects simple C1 feedstocks to the photoredox substrates used by Patel et al. (2015), Xu et al. (2018), and Ritson and Sutherland (2023). Atmospheric production does not automatically establish the necessary pond concentrations; evaporation, retention and delivery are boundary conditions rather than demonstrated outcomes of this edge.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiophosphate photoreduction with HCN capture to serine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Thiophosphate is a consumed photoreductant, represented by mol_012; UV is the energy source, not a catalyst.\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K; nominal ambient model window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 6.5; glycolonitrile and thiophosphate around 20 mM in the cited experimental family; added HCN; irradiation near 254-256 nm; no externally supplied ammonia is required for this branch.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoreduction accesses an imine-level intermediate from glycolonitrile; HCN capture forms the aminonitrile while retaining nitrile-derived nitrogen in the amino functionality. Phosphate and elemental sulfur are a provisional net oxidation closure for the donor.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson and Sutherland (2023) reported approximately 16% serine nitrile after one hour with HCN present, providing strong evidence for the organic transformation and HCN-derived nitrogen incorporation. The phosphate/S8 donor-product closure is speculative bookkeeping, not a measured exclusive product distribution in the supplied report. Natural thiophosphate inventories, reagent concentration and solar-versus-lamp rates remain unresolved; surface access to these wavelengths is supported by Ranjan and Sasselov (2017).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiophosphate photoreduction of glycolonitrile followed by imine hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Thiophosphate is a stoichiometric photoreductant, not a mineral catalyst.\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310 K; nominal ambient model window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Approximately pH 6.5; around 20 mM glycolonitrile and thiophosphate; UV near 254-256 nm; hydrolysis competes with cyanide trapping of reduced intermediates.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reduction of the nitrile gives an imine-level species whose hydrolysis releases glycolaldehyde and ammonia/ammonium. Donor oxidation is provisionally closed as phosphate plus elemental sulfur.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ritson and Sutherland (2023) reported approximately 19% glycolaldehyde after one hour, increasing to roughly 34% with phosphate buffering. These are glycolaldehyde yields, not serine yields. Release of the nitrile nitrogen on hydrolysis follows the proposed mechanism; the phosphate/S8 closure remains an unverified donor-side simplification. Subsequent alkaline Strecker chemistry or bisulfite storage requires a separate processing stage.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfite-ferrocyanide photoreductive supply of glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Dissolved ferrocyanide/cyanometallate photoredox system\",\n        \"Sulfite, represented by mol_010, is a consumed reductant rather than a catalyst.\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; nominal ambient model window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Dissolved ferrocyanide-associated photoredox chemistry\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic UV exposure near 254 nm; concentrated cyanohydrin/S(IV) solution. The cited overall experiment used approximately 100 mM formaldehyde, 200 mM cyanide and 200 mM sulfite.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoredox reduction accesses a glycolonitrile-derived imine; hydrolysis supplies glycolaldehyde. Sulfate represents formal two-electron oxidation of the S(IV) donor.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Xu et al. (2018) demonstrated sulfite/ferrocyanide reductive homologation and products requiring glycolaldehyde-level chemistry. This edge extracts the aldehyde-supply module rather than claiming a reported isolated glycolaldehyde yield. Sulfate is a provisional net donor-product closure; dithionate or other sulfur products may also form. Ferrocyanide is dissolved coordination chemistry, not evidence for a catalytic iron-mineral surface.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-cyanide and sulfide photoreductive supply of glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_015\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Dissolved Cu(I)/Cu(II)-cyanide photoredox system\",\n        \"Hydrogen sulfide/sulfide, represented by mol_015, is the consumed reductant.\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; nominal ambient model window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Copper-cyanide photoredox cycling\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic, UV-exposed cyanide/sulfide water; controlled dissolved copper availability. Excess sulfide can precipitate copper sulfides, so soluble catalyst availability is not automatic.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoredox reduction of glycolonitrile followed by hydrolysis produces glycolaldehyde and ammonia; oxidation of sulfide is represented by an elemental-sulfur sink.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) support the sulfide/copper-cyanide reductive homologation framework. The network uses its aldehyde supply, then a separately supported Strecker module, rather than asserting a selective one-pot serine synthesis. Elemental sulfur is a reasonable formal oxidized-sulfur sink, but the complete sulfur-product distribution and copper speciation are not determined here.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfite-ferrocyanide photochemical formation of serine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Dissolved ferrocyanide-associated photoredox system\",\n        \"Sulfite is a stoichiometric donor, not a catalyst.\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320 K; nominal ambient model window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Dissolved ferrocyanide-associated photoredox chemistry\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic irradiation near 254 nm for approximately 12-12.5 hours in the reported experimental family; high formaldehyde/cyanide/sulfite inventories; competing glyceronitrile formation is substantial.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A net reductive homologation and cyanide-trapping module produces serine aminonitrile. Imine capture is a plausible molecular interpretation; competing imine hydrolysis and nitrogen exchange are not resolved by this graph.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Xu et al. (2018) directly quantified approximately 4% serine nitrile alongside approximately 26% glyceronitrile and other products. The detected precursor is well supported, but this compact edge is not proof of an exclusively nitrogen-retaining microscopic mechanism. Sulfate is a provisional donor-side closure rather than a verified exclusive sulfur product. The reported precursor yield must not be transferred to free serine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Formaldehyde Strecker formation of glycine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic aqueous formaldehyde, ammonia/ammonium and HCN/cyanide; mildly alkaline conditions; avoid prolonged carbonyl residence before capture.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formaldehyde/ammonia condensation followed by cyanide addition forms aminoacetonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Miller (1957) supports the classical aldehyde Strecker mechanism. Kamaluddin et al. (1979) independently observed aminoacetonitrile before amino acids in formaldehyde/hydroxylamine chemistry, although their full modified-seawater experiment is not reproduced here. This edge supplies glycine from C1 feedstocks without assuming pre-existing glycine or a specific mineral catalyst.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycine aminonitrile hydration to glycinamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_023\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved acid provides nitrile-hydration catalysis.\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-363 K; proposed natural-hydrolysis window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"General acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Acidic aqueous residence; rate at naturally achievable acidity remains an integration uncertainty.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydration of the nitrile gives the corresponding primary amide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kamaluddin et al. (1979) observed aminoacetonitrile and glycinamide before amino-acid accumulation, supporting these molecular intermediates. That experiment used a hotter, metal-enriched medium; transfer to the cooler acid-catalyzed conditions specified here is an explicit extrapolation. Miller (1957) supports the broader aminonitrile-hydrolysis route.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycinamide hydrolysis to glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_023\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Dissolved acid provides amide-hydrolysis catalysis.\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-363 K; proposed natural-hydrolysis window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"General acid catalysis\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Prolonged aqueous hydrolysis, followed by adjustment to the milder conditions of glycine hydroxymethylation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis converts the carboxamide group to a carboxyl group and releases ammonia/ammonium.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the conventional completion of glycine Strecker synthesis, consistent with Miller (1957) and the precursor ordering reported by Kamaluddin et al. (1979). The graph does not claim that their modified-seawater catalysts are necessary, or that the proposed cooler environmental rate was measured.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Nonenzymatic glycine hydroxymethylation to serine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_024\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-353 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Homogeneous weakly acidic water, pH 3.3-3.7; no solid phase required. Other reported regimes include 323 K at pH 8 and 343 K at pH 6.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net carbon-alpha hydroxymethylation adds the formaldehyde carbon to the glycine skeleton. The reacting solution also supports imine exchange, transamination and nitrogen methylation; a uniquely established elementary mechanism is not assumed.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ivanov and Ivanov (1983) demonstrated serine formation from glycine/formaldehyde at 60-80 degrees Celsius without inorganic solids. Mohammed et al. (2017) identified serine within a broader glycine/formaldehyde network under milder conditions. This is a genuine alternative serine-forming transformation, but poor selectivity and competing N-methylation/transamination limit effective yield. Its upstream glycine supply in this network still uses cyanide.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_020\",\n      \"name\": \"Competing dehydration of N-formylserine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_019\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_025\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Formamide-rich medium modifies the reaction environment but is not assigned as a catalyst.\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-370 K; exploratory surface range, not an established rate window\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Low water activity and heating favor elimination relative to hydrolytic recovery. The supplied summary does not provide the precise experimental dehydration temperature.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Loss of side-chain hydroxyl and an alpha hydrogen forms the carbon-carbon double bond of the N-formylated dehydroalanine nitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Green and Russell (2023) reported dehydration chemistry of the serine-derived N-formyl aminonitrile at elevated temperature. Its rate within the stated terrestrial surface window is not established. This loss branch is included because N-formylation can divert material away from free serine rather than simply protect it.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_021\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal serine loss through inferred dehydration\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\n        \"Pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite assemblage as a redox buffer, not an established serine-dehydration catalyst\",\n        \"K-feldspar-muscovite-quartz assemblage as a proton-activity buffer in the cited experiments\"\n      ],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 473 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic hot water with mineral-buffered redox and proton activity; represents undesirable prolonged exposure, not the proposed production environment.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Candidate dehydration of serine gives dehydroalanine as a transient intermediate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Andersson and Holm (2000) observed rapid serine destruction and alanine/glycine formation under these hydrothermal conditions. Dehydration through dehydroalanine is a mechanistic interpretation, not a claim that this intermediate was isolated or its standalone rate measured. The branch shows why high-temperature vents are not assumed to preserve surface-produced serine.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_022\",\n      \"name\": \"Inferred reductive conversion of dehydroalanine to alanine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_026\", \"mol_027\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_028\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Reducing pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite-buffered fluid; surface-specific catalytic responsibility is unresolved.\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"Approximately 473 K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Approximately 50 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Hydrogen represents the net reducing reservoir. Actual electron/proton transfer may involve mineral-buffered dissolved species rather than direct uncatalyzed addition of molecular hydrogen.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net reduction of the dehydroalanine double bond gives alanine, irreversibly losing serine's hydroxyl-bearing identity within this network.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Andersson and Holm (2000) support the overall serine-to-alanine loss under reducing hydrothermal conditions. Resolution into dehydration followed by hydrogen-equivalent reduction is mechanistically inferred and remains speculative as an independently validated elementary sequence. No selective serine-synthesis catalyst is implied.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiophosphate photochemistry with HCN-derived amino nitrogen\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde and HCN generate glycolonitrile, followed by the experimentally supported thiophosphate/HCN serine-aminonitrile module and separately proposed natural hydrolysis. No external ammonia is needed for precursor formation. The reported approximately 16% yield concerns the aminonitrile, not free serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiophosphate-derived glycolaldehyde followed by direct Strecker chemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"Photochemical reduction and hydrolysis first expose glycolaldehyde. A separate ammonia-containing alkaline stage forms serine aminonitrile, followed by hydrolysis. This differs from P1 by allowing reduced nitrile nitrogen to leave the aldehyde-supply module before amino-nitrogen incorporation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Thiophosphate precursor supply with bisulfite storage\",\n      \"description\": \"Thiophosphate-derived glycolaldehyde is trapped before concentration or storage, converted to the stable ammonia-bisulfite adduct, and then captured by cyanide. Storage has an experimentally supported chemical purpose, but integration of the photochemical and protected modules has not been demonstrated as one uninterrupted natural sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfite-ferrocyanide photochemical aminonitrile pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"The sulfite/ferrocyanide reductive-homologation module supplies serine aminonitrile directly within a mixed product network. Subsequent hydration and amide hydrolysis remain separate environmental requirements. The approximately 4% experimental precursor yield is not a free-serine yield.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_015\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfite-ferrocyanide aldehyde supply followed by direct Strecker chemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"Photoreductive homologation is used as a glycolaldehyde source rather than relying on its minor direct serine-nitrile branch. Subsequent ammonia/cyanide capture and hydrolysis occur under different conditions. Aldehyde recovery and compatibility between stages are unvalidated integration requirements.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Sulfite-ferrocyanide aldehyde supply with protected storage\",\n      \"description\": \"Glycolaldehyde generated in the sulfite photoredox system enters a bisulfite storage branch before ammonia and cyanide processing. The common S(IV) inventory has distinct roles as consumed photoreductant and reversible carbonyl-trapping reagent; this does not imply that one fixed pH optimizes both roles.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-sulfide photochemical supply followed by direct Strecker chemistry\",\n      \"description\": \"Copper-cyanide/sulfide photochemistry supplies glycolaldehyde from the C1-derived cyanohydrin. Subsequent Strecker chemistry uses ammonia and cyanide, followed by the common hydrolysis bottleneck. Dissolved copper availability and sulfide precipitation constrain this source module.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-sulfide precursor supply with bisulfite-mediated preservation\",\n      \"description\": \"The copper/sulfide source module is followed by a chemically motivated storage interval using an independently supplied S(IV) reservoir. The protected aldehyde is then converted to serine aminonitrile and hydrolyzed. Compatibility of the sulfide-containing source solution with later bisulfite storage is a testable integration assumption, not an established one-pot result.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_016\", \"mol_017\", \"mol_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Photochemical precursor supply through N-formylated serine derivatives\",\n      \"description\": \"Thiophosphate-derived glycolaldehyde supplies serine aminonitrile, which enters experimentally supported N-formylation chemistry in concentrated formamide. Completion through N-formylserine and deformylation is speculative and competes with dehydration. This is the lowest-confidence target-producing pathway in the network.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_001\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_019\", \"mol_020\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycine synthesis followed by formaldehyde hydroxymethylation\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde, HCN and ammonia first supply glycine through its aminonitrile and amide. A second formaldehyde-derived carbon is then incorporated by the experimentally demonstrated glycine-to-serine reaction. Unlike the other pathways, the serine-forming carbon-carbon bond is made by glycine hydroxymethylation rather than glycolaldehyde Strecker chemistry.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_016\", \"rxn_017\", \"rxn_018\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_022\", \"mol_023\", \"mol_024\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\n    \"mol_005\",\n    \"mol_006\",\n    \"mol_007\",\n    \"mol_009\",\n    \"mol_019\"\n  ],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\", \"rxn_015\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_012\", \"rxn_013\", \"rxn_014\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_004\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_015\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_019\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_021\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_020\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Serine, without stereochemical selection", "overall_strategy": "Construct a target-centered network from formaldehyde, HCN, ammonia and explicitly identified auxiliary reagents. Ten distinct reaction sequences combine experimentally supported photochemical precursor supply, direct or bisulfite-protected Strecker chemistry, a lower-confidence formamide branch, and glycine hydroxymethylation. These are ten network pathways, not ten independent reaction families or ten experimentally demonstrated continuous syntheses. Photochemical production of serine aminonitrile is distinguished from its subsequent, environmentally less constrained hydrolysis to free serine. Different pH stages require sequential processing rather than one homogeneous reactor. Neutral-parent formulas represent acid-base families; actual cyanide, sulfite, thiophosphate and ammonium speciation is specified in the conditions. Sulfate, phosphate and elemental sulfur provide provisional, atom-balanced donor-side closures for photoredox modules; their exclusive formation is not established by the supplied studies. These closures must not be used to infer measured donor efficiencies. The network includes experimentally motivated destruction branches and exceeds the requested minimum size to represent intermediates and ten pathways explicitly. Unresolved polymeric precursors are not assigned invented molecular structures. No molecular oxygen or biological catalyst is required, and racemic serine is expected.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "Water", "common_name": "Water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "Methanal", "common_name": "Formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mo