 an imine or iminium species; cyanide addition establishes the serine carbon skeleton. Water is the condensation coproduct.", "reasoning": "This is the best-resolved carbon-skeleton construction for serine. Miller (1957) established the general aldehyde/HCN Strecker mechanism, and Ritson et al. (2018) demonstrated rapid serine-aminonitrile formation in the protected glycolaldehyde system. The reported approximately two-hour conversion applies specifically to that protected experiment, not necessarily to every unprotected mixture. Glycolaldehyde degradation competes, and no mineral catalyst is required."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Hydration of serine aminonitrile to serinamide", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved proton activity provides general acid catalysis; no mineral is assigned."], "conditions": {"temperature": "333-363 K; proposed natural-hydrolysis window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "General acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Transfer to acidic water, provisionally pH 1-3; prolonged residence; reduced UV exposure through depth or shading. This is not a demonstrated rate prescription."}, "mechanism": "Acid-assisted water addition to the nitrile gives an imidic intermediate that tautomerizes to the primary amide.", "reasoning": "An amide stage is chemically expected in nitrile hydrolysis. Miller (1957) supports aminonitrile hydrolysis in discharge-product chemistry, while Xu et al. (2018) and Ritson and Sutherland (2023) establish serine aminonitrile rather than a continuous mild route to free serine. The explicit serinamide node and proposed natural conditions are mechanistic extrapolations, not a reported isolated intermediate and measured environmental rate. This is a principal network bottleneck."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Serinamide hydrolysis to free serine", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved proton activity provides general acid catalysis."], "conditions": {"temperature": "333-363 K; proposed natural-hydrolysis window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "General acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic aqueous residence followed by cooling; ammonia product is predominantly ammonium at the proposed pH. Residence time and serine survival require experimental determination."}, "mechanism": "Water attacks the protonated amide carbonyl; collapse of the tetrahedral intermediate releases ammonia/ammonium and forms the carboxyl group.", "reasoning": "This completes conventional aminonitrile-to-amino-acid hydrolysis, consistent with Miller (1957). Its integration with modern serine-nitrile photochemistry is not established by the supplied experiments, so the specified natural completion remains an extrapolation. Analytical strong-acid hydrolysis must not be equated with this environmental scenario. Cooling limits the destruction documented by Andersson and Holm (2000)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Reversible bisulfite storage of glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_016"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Aqueous bisulfite is a stoichiometric reversible trapping reagent, represented by mol_010, not a catalyst."], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350 K; surface concentration and storage scenario", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 6.5; aqueous capture with optional evaporation and rewetting. Formulas use neutral-parent representations of the actual ionic adducts."}, "mechanism": "Bisulfite adds nucleophilically to the aldehyde carbonyl to form a hydroxysulfonate reservoir.", "reasoning": "Ritson et al. (2018) recovered more than 90% of glycolaldehyde as its adduct or hydrate after their protected dry-down/reconstitution procedure, unlike the unprotected control. This branch has a chemical storage function rather than merely adding connectivity. Compatibility with residual upstream photoredox reagents must still be tested."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Ammonia incorporation into the protected glycolaldehyde reservoir", "inputs": ["mol_016", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_017", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K; proposed ambient window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Raise the protected solution to approximately pH 9.2 with available ammonia/ammonium buffering; maintain anoxic storage."}, "mechanism": "Reversible carbonyl-adduct exchange, imine formation and sulfite capture establish the amino-hydroxysulfonate. The equation is a net transformation, not a claim of concerted substitution.", "reasoning": "Ritson et al. (2018) identified a stable 1-amino-2-hydroxyethylsulfonate reservoir in glycolaldehyde/ammonia/bisulfite chemistry, persisting during a seven-day storage test. This separates preservation from subsequent cyanide addition without assigning an unsupported mineral catalyst."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Cyanide conversion of the protected amino adduct to serine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_017", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K; proposed ambient window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 9.2 after cyanide addition; the cited experiment reported smooth conversion within about two hours."}, "mechanism": "Reversible sulfite loss exposes an imine/iminium equivalent that is trapped by cyanide, regenerating the dissolved S(IV) reservoir.", "reasoning": "This is directly supported by Ritson et al. (2018). Bisulfite acts as a reversible protecting reagent and is released during product formation; it is not a heterogeneous catalyst. Efficient formation of serine aminonitrile does not establish subsequent free-serine accumulation."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "N-Formylation of serine aminonitrile in formamide", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_018"], "outputs": ["mol_019", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Formamide is the concentrated medium and formyl donor, represented by mol_018, not a catalyst."], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370 K; candidate surface window, with kinetics requiring verification", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "Formamide-rich, low-water-activity phase after concentration; dissolved salts and residual water may remain. The supplied summary does not specify the study's exact temperature."}, "mechanism": "Net transfer of a formyl group from formamide to the aminonitrile amino group releases ammonia.", "reasoning": "Green and Russell (2023) demonstrated N-formylated serine-nitrile chemistry in formamide. The derivative is therefore a supported molecular branch, but concentration of formamide at a natural site and operation throughout the proposed surface window remain uncertain. N-Formylation is not evidence that the derivative subsequently yields free serine efficiently."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Candidate hydrolysis of N-formylserine aminonitrile to N-formylserine", "inputs": ["mol_019", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_020", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved acid provides hydrolysis catalysis."], "conditions": {"temperature": "333-363 K; speculative environmental operating window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "General acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Rehydration into acidic water, provisionally pH 1-3; prolonged residence. The ordering and selectivity of nitrile hydrolysis versus deformylation are unknown."}, "mechanism": "Net nitrile-to-carboxylic-acid hydrolysis through an amide stage, with temporary retention of the N-formyl group.", "reasoning": "Speculative completion of the precursor chemistry demonstrated by Green and Russell (2023). Ordinary nitrile hydrolysis makes this chemically possible, but the supplied research does not establish selective accumulation of N-formylserine from this substrate under natural conditions. Deformylation, dehydration and other losses may outrun this branch; it is included as a testable route, not a demonstrated serine yield."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Candidate deformylation of N-formylserine", "inputs": ["mol_020", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_021"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved acid catalyzes cleavage of the formamide linkage."], "conditions": {"temperature": "333-363 K; speculative environmental operating window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "General acid catalysis", "aqueous": true, "other": "Acidic rehydration followed by cooling; no claim of measured rate or selective serine recovery."}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic cleavage of the N-formyl amide releases serine and formic acid.", "reasoning": "Chemically conventional deprotection, but speculative as the environmental endpoint of the Green and Russell (2023) route. The supplied work supports the N-formyl aminonitrile precursor, not this complete mild sequence to free serine. This step explicitly prevents a protected derivative from being mislabeled as the target."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Formaldehyde cyanohydrin formation", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic aqueous formaldehyde/cyanide inventory; mildly acidic to mildly alkaline conditions with sufficient cyanide nucleophile; concentration and HCN volatilization are important."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide adds to formaldehyde, followed by proton transfer, producing the C2 cyanohydrin glycolonitrile.", "reasoning": "This established entry reaction connects simple C1 feedstocks to the photoredox substrates used by Patel et al. (2015), Xu et al. (2018), and Ritson and Sutherland (2023). Atmospheric production does not automatically establish the necessary pond concentrations; evaporation, retention and delivery are boundary conditions rather than demonstrated outcomes of this edge."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Thiophosphate photoreduction with HCN capture to serine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_003", "mol_012", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_013", "mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Thiophosphate is a consumed photoreductant, represented by mol_012; UV is the energy source, not a catalyst."], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K; nominal ambient model window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 6.5; glycolonitrile and thiophosphate around 20 mM in the cited experimental family; added HCN; irradiation near 254-256 nm; no externally supplied ammonia is required for this branch."}, "mechanism": "Photoreduction accesses an imine-level intermediate from glycolonitrile; HCN capture forms the aminonitrile while retaining nitrile-derived nitrogen in the amino functionality. Phosphate and elemental sulfur are a provisional net oxidation closure for the donor.", "reasoning": "Ritson and Sutherland (2023) reported approximately 16% serine nitrile after one hour with HCN present, providing strong evidence for the organic transformation and HCN-derived nitrogen incorporation. The phosphate/S8 donor-product closure is speculative bookkeeping, not a measured exclusive product distribution in the supplied report. Natural thiophosphate inventories, reagent concentration and solar-versus-lamp rates remain unresolved; surface access to these wavelengths is supported by Ranjan and Sasselov (2017)."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Thiophosphate photoreduction of glycolonitrile followed by imine hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_012", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004", "mol_013", "mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Thiophosphate is a stoichiometric photoreductant, not a mineral catalyst."], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310 K; nominal ambient model window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Approximately pH 6.5; around 20 mM glycolonitrile and thiophosphate; UV near 254-256 nm; hydrolysis competes with cyanide trapping of reduced intermediates."}, "mechanism": "Reduction of the nitrile gives an imine-level species whose hydrolysis releases glycolaldehyde and ammonia/ammonium. Donor oxidation is provisionally closed as phosphate plus elemental sulfur.", "reasoning": "Ritson and Sutherland (2023) reported approximately 19% glycolaldehyde after one hour, increasing to roughly 34% with phosphate buffering. These are glycolaldehyde yields, not serine yields. Release of the nitrile nitrogen on hydrolysis follows the proposed mechanism; the phosphate/S8 closure remains an unverified donor-side simplification. Subsequent alkaline Strecker chemistry or bisulfite storage requires a separate processing stage."}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Sulfite-ferrocyanide photoreductive supply of glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_010", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004", "mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved ferrocyanide/cyanometallate photoredox system", "Sulfite, represented by mol_010, is a consumed reductant rather than a catalyst."], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K; nominal ambient model window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Dissolved ferrocyanide-associated photoredox chemistry", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic UV exposure near 254 nm; concentrated cyanohydrin/S(IV) solution. The cited overall experiment used approximately 100 mM formaldehyde, 200 mM cyanide and 200 mM sulfite."}, "mechanism": "Photoredox reduction accesses a glycolonitrile-derived imine; hydrolysis supplies glycolaldehyde. Sulfate represents formal two-electron oxidation of the S(IV) donor.", "reasoning": "Xu et al. (2018) demonstrated sulfite/ferrocyanide reductive homologation and products requiring glycolaldehyde-level chemistry. This edge extracts the aldehyde-supply module rather than claiming a reported isolated glycolaldehyde yield. Sulfate is a provisional net donor-product closure; dithionate or other sulfur products may also form. Ferrocyanide is dissolved coordination chemistry, not evidence for a catalytic iron-mineral surface."}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Copper-cyanide and sulfide photoreductive supply of glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_015", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004", "mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved Cu(I)/Cu(II)-cyanide photoredox system", "Hydrogen sulfide/sulfide, represented by mol_015, is the consumed reductant."], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K; nominal ambient model window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Copper-cyanide photoredox cycling", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic, UV-exposed cyanide/sulfide water; controlled dissolved copper availability. Excess sulfide can precipitate copper sulfides, so soluble catalyst availability is not automatic."}, "mechanism": "Photoredox reduction of glycolonitrile followed by hydrolysis produces glycolaldehyde and ammonia; oxidation of sulfide is represented by an elemental-sulfur sink.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) support the sulfide/copper-cyanide reductive homologation framework. The network uses its aldehyde supply, then a separately supported Strecker module, rather than asserting a selective one-pot serine synthesis. Elemental sulfur is a reasonable formal oxidized-sulfur sink, but the complete sulfur-product distribution and copper speciation are not determined here."}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Sulfite-ferrocyanide photochemical formation of serine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_003", "mol_010", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Dissolved ferrocyanide-associated photoredox system", "Sulfite is a stoichiometric donor, not a catalyst."], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320 K; nominal ambient model window", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": "Dissolved ferrocyanide-associated photoredox chemistry", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic irradiation near 254 nm for approximately 12-12.5 hours in the reported experimental family; high formaldehyde/cyanide/sulfite inventories; competing glyceronitrile formation is substantial."}, "mechanism": "A net reductive homologation and cyanide-trapping module produces serine aminonitrile. Imine capture is a plausible molecular interpretation; competing imine hydrolysis and nitrogen exchange are not resolved by this graph.", "reasoning": "Xu et al. (2018) directly quantified approximately 4% serine nitrile alongside approximately 26% glyceronitrile and other products. The detected precursor is well supported, but this compact edge is not proof of an exclusively nitrogen-retaining microscopic mechanism. Sulfate is a provisional donor-side closure rather than a verified exclusive sulfur product. The reported precursor yield must not be transferred to free serine."}, {"id": "rxn_016", "name": "Formaldehyde Strecker formation of glycine aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_002", "mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_022", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "Approximately 1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic aqueous formaldehyde, ammonia/ammonium and HCN/cyanide; mildly alkaline conditions; avoid prolonged carbonyl residence before capture."}, "mechanism": "Formaldehyde/ammonia condensation followed by cyanide addition forms aminoacetonitrile.", "reasoning": "Miller (1957) supports the classical aldehyde Strecker mechanism. Kamaluddin et al. (1979) independently observed aminoacetonitrile before amino acids in formaldehyde/hydroxylami