uble bond of the N-formylated dehydroalanine nitrile.", "reasoning": "Green and Russell (2023) reported dehydration chemistry of the serine-derived N-formyl aminonitrile at elevated temperature. Its rate within the stated terrestrial surface window is not established. This loss branch is included because N-formylation can divert material away from free serine rather than simply protect it."}, {"id": "rxn_021", "name": "Hydrothermal serine loss through inferred dehydration", "inputs": ["mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_026", "mol_001"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite assemblage as a redox buffer, not an established serine-dehydration catalyst", "K-feldspar-muscovite-quartz assemblage as a proton-activity buffer in the cited experiments"], "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 473 K", "pressure": "Approximately 50 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic hot water with mineral-buffered redox and proton activity; represents undesirable prolonged exposure, not the proposed production environment."}, "mechanism": "Candidate dehydration of serine gives dehydroalanine as a transient intermediate.", "reasoning": "Andersson and Holm (2000) observed rapid serine destruction and alanine/glycine formation under these hydrothermal conditions. Dehydration through dehydroalanine is a mechanistic interpretation, not a claim that this intermediate was isolated or its standalone rate measured. The branch shows why high-temperature vents are not assumed to preserve surface-produced serine."}, {"id": "rxn_022", "name": "Inferred reductive conversion of dehydroalanine to alanine", "inputs": ["mol_026", "mol_027"], "outputs": ["mol_028"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Reducing pyrite-pyrrhotite-magnetite-buffered fluid; surface-specific catalytic responsibility is unresolved."], "conditions": {"temperature": "Approximately 473 K", "pressure": "Approximately 50 bar", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Hydrog