ence-based precursor protection, but its behavior in a thiophosphate-derived mixture and the required pH history remain unspecified. All productive transformations can plausibly occur in surface settings; the broad environment label alone does not establish their operating compatibility.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Demonstrate bisulfite trapping and protected Strecker conversion using an actual thiophosphate photochemical mixture.\",\n        \"Specify storage, pH adjustment and reagent activities, including acid-base speciation.\",\n        \"Show that subsequent hydrolysis produces recoverable free serine and does not simply redistribute material among nitrile, amide and degradation products.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.89,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.75,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.65\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.83,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.9,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.73,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.73,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.8,\n      \"feedback\": \"Xu et al. (2018) directly reported approximately 4% serine aminonitrile within a broader sulfite/ferrocyanide photochemical product mixture. This supports the central precursor-forming branch and does not require inventing an unbalanced ammonia source. Nevertheless, rxn_009 is a lumped photochemical transformation rather than a fully resolved elementary mechanism. Neither that precursor yield nor the balanced equation demonstrates free-serine production. The downstream hydrolysis sequence and natural irradiation/concentration regime remain insufficiently specified.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Annotate rxn_009 as an experimentally supported net branch with the reported substrate mixture, irradiation conditions and product distribution.\",\n        \"Quantify serine-aminonitrile recovery and competing reactions in the retained photochemical matrix.\",\n        \"Demonstrate surface-compatible nitrile and amide hydrolysis, distinguishing directly present serine from serine released during analytical work-up.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.95,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.94,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.75,\n        \"rxn_012\": 0.65\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.84,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.92,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.76,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.74,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.82,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is the best-supported target-precursor branch: Ritson and Sutherland (2023) reported approximately 16% serine aminonitrile after one hour with HCN present in thiophosphate photochemistry. Its nitrogen accounting is sound without externally added ammonia. The experimental result provides unusually direct support for rxn_010, but remains a nitrile result rather than a free-serine result. The selection of this pathway as best rests on that substrate-specific evidence, not its step count or reagent identity. Hydrolysis and preservation still prevent high-confidence end-to-end approval.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_011\",\n        \"rxn_012\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Assign explicit operating conditions, distinguishing the published millimolar, pH-6.5, 254-256-nm experiment from any proposed natural photon exposure and concentrations.\",\n        \"Characterize sulfur/phosphorus coproducts before using the formal phosphate/S8 outputs in quantitative redox or kinetic models.\",\n        \"Demonstrate serinamide formation and hydrolysis with a measured free-serine mass balance and simultaneous loss measurements.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_013\": 0.93,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.82,\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.75,\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.81\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.83,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.86,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.73,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.79,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.8,\n      \"feedback\": \"Classical glycine Strecker synthesis and hydrolysis provide a credible precursor route. Ivanov and Ivanov (1983) and Mohammed et al. (2017) support serine formation from glycine and formaldehyde, so rxn_016 is more than a formal carbon-balance proposal. Importantly, this pathway does not depend on the serinamide bottleneck shared by P1-P8. Its limitations are unassigned glycine-precursor hydrolysis conditions and the selectivity of hydroxymethylation. The explicit rxn_018 transamination competitor is appropriate, but additional formaldehyde-driven side chemistry remains relevant.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_014\",\n        \"rxn_015\",\n        \"rxn_016\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Specify and validate glycine-aminonitrile and glycinamide hydrolysis conditions before the formaldehyde reaction.\",\n        \"Select a documented hydroxymethylation operating window, such as the reported mildly acidic 60-80-degree-C experiments, rather than leaving rxn_016 as generic surface chemistry.\",\n        \"Measure free-serine yield against transamination, N-methylation and other formaldehyde-consuming reactions in the complete mixture.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_017\": 0.43,\n        \"rxn_014\": 0.82,\n        \"rxn_015\": 0.75,\n        \"rxn_016\": 0.81\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.65,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.64,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.62,\n  